**Title:**  
Conditional Multifractal Priors for Data‑Efficient, Interpretable Screening of Diabetic Retinopathy: Theory, Architecture, and Regulatory Design

**Author:**  
Ouadi Maakoul

---

### Abstract

Diabetic retinopathy (DR) screening demands models that are data‑efficient, interpretable, and deployable on low‑cost edge devices. This thesis develops a complete framework—theory, architecture, and regulatory design—that conditions a compact multimodal network on 18‑dimensional multifractal‑lacunarity features to achieve sample‑complexity reduction, clinical interpretability, and built‑in reliability flagging.

We prove a Lipschitz‑free PAC‑Bayesian generalisation bound where the sample complexity scales with the data‑dependent upper Minkowski (box‑counting) dimension \(D_f\) of the posterior parameter set, rather than with the ambient parameter count \(p\). Crucially, we explicitly address the failure modes of fractal generalisation metrics recently identified by Tan et al. (NeurIPS 2024): the bound is complemented by an operational condition that monitors the distance from a near‑optimal initialisation \(\|w - w_0\|^2\) and triggers a self‑diagnostic `UNRELIABLE` flag when the theoretical guarantees become vacuous, thereby handling the sensitivity of persistent‑homology proxies to poor initialisation.

The feature vector \(\phi\) combines 2D‑MF‑DFA generalised Hurst exponents \(h(q)\) (\(q=-5,\dots,5\)), the box‑counting dimension \(D_0\), the multifractal spectrum width \(\Delta\alpha\), and gliding‑box lacunarity \(\Lambda(r)\) at three spatial scales. We justify that \(\Delta\alpha\) and \(\Lambda(r)\) capture complementary aspects of microvascular heterogeneity—empirical correlation \(\rho < 0.3\) on retinal masks—and that the 2D‑MF‑DFA algorithm with second‑order detrending yields stable, clinically meaningful estimates (\(D_0 = 1.48 \pm 0.05\) for healthy retinas, falling to \(1.35 \pm 0.06\) in severe NPDR).

The FracFormer v3 architecture fuses a fundus image and an OCT‑A en‑face slab via cross‑attention to the \(\phi\) vector. After quantisation‑aware training, the model occupies 8.3 MB (INT8) and runs in 20 ms on a Jetson Orin. It includes a dynamic threshold calibration that reduces false positives by 71.4% (following EdgeRetina) and auxiliary heads that reconstruct \(\phi\) and estimate \(\|w - w_0\|^2\).

From the outset, the design is aligned with regulatory requirements. We classify the system as a Computer‑Aided Detection (CADe) device under 21 CFR 892.2090 (product code POK), provide a Predetermined Change Control Plan (PCCP) compliant with the FDA’s August 2025 guidance, and map a compliance roadmap for the EU AI Act (high‑risk, Annex I, deadline 2 August 2028). All validation is computational on public datasets (EyePACS, MESSIDOR‑2, OCTA‑500, OCT5k, DM1‑156) under existing research licenses; no human subjects or physical experiments are involved.

