### C.1 Scope and Purpose

This Predetermined Change Control Plan describes the types of planned modifications to FracFormer that may be implemented after initial clearance, together with the verification and validation activities that will be performed prior to release of the modified device. The PCCP is organised into four categories of modifications, each assigned an impact level in accordance with FDA’s risk‑based framework. All validation relies on publicly available datasets and standardised performance metrics; no new clinical investigations are required for low‑ and moderate‑impact changes unless otherwise indicated.

### C.2 Planned Modifications

| Modification ID | Description | Category |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| PCCP‑1 | Addition of a new OCT‑A acquisition device (different field of view, vendor, or resolution) | Data acquisition |
| PCCP‑2 | Extension of the MFDFA moment range from \(q \in [-5,5]\) to \(q \in [-7,7]\) | Algorithm update |
| PCCP‑3 | Retraining of the model with up to 30 % additional training data | Model update |
| PCCP‑4 | Replacement of the OCT‑A encoder (ViT‑Small/16) with ViT‑Tiny | Architecture update |

### C.3 Modification‑Specific Verification, Validation, and Impact Assessment

#### PCCP‑1: New OCT‑A Device

- **Verification:**  
  - Acquire a minimum of 100 representative OCT‑A volumes from the new device.  
  - Run the full FracFormer pipeline; confirm that vessel segmentation completes without error and that the multifractal–lacunarity feature extraction returns valid floating‑point values (no NaN, no negative lacunarity).  
  - If vessel masks are manually annotated for a subset (≥ 20 volumes), compute the Dice similarity coefficient; require Dice ≥ 0.70.

- **Validation:**  
  - Assemble a dataset of at least 2,000 images (fundus + OCT‑A pairs) from the new device, with ground‑truth DR grades adjudicated by a qualified ophthalmologist.  
  - Compare the area under the ROC curve (AUC) for referable DR (R1–R4 vs. R0) against the performance of the original cleared device on its reference test set.  
  - Acceptance criterion: \(\Delta\text{AUC} < 0.02\) and sensitivity at 95 % specificity ≥ 90 %.

- **Impact Assessment:**  
  - **Impact level:** Low. The fractal feature extraction is device‑agnostic; only the OCT‑A encoder input statistics may shift. No retraining is required. The modification does not alter the device’s fundamental algorithm or intended use.

#### PCCP‑2: Extended MFDFA \(q\)-Range

- **Verification:**  
  - Recompute the 18‑dimensional feature vector \(\phi\) on the MESSIDOR‑2 validation split (500 images) using the new \(q\)-range \([-7,7]\).  
  - Plot \(h(q)\) vs. \(q\); verify that the curve is smooth and that no numerical instabilities (singular matrices, overflow) occur for \(q = \pm 6, \pm 7\).  
  - Check that the newly computed \(\Delta\alpha\) differs from the original by less than 10 % of its reference range.

- **Validation:**  
  - Re‑run the external validation on the DM1‑156 cohort (Section 6.5) with the updated \(\phi\) definition.  
  - Acceptance criterion: sensitivity for NPDR detection (mild/moderate/severe vs. healthy) remains ≥ 83 %, i.e., non‑inferior to the 84 % accuracy reported by Alam et al. (2020).

- **Impact Assessment:**  
  - **Impact level:** Moderate. The change modifies the input features to the network and may affect the learned representation. Retrospective re‑validation on DM1‑156 is sufficient to demonstrate maintained performance; no new prospective data collection is mandated because the modification is an algorithmic refinement within the same clinical indication.

#### PCCP‑3: Retraining with Additional Data

- **Verification:**  
  - Combine the original training set with the new data (up to 30 % increase in total size).  
  - Run the full training pipeline using the same hyperparameters and random seeds as the original submission.  
  - Verify that the training loss converges within the pre‑specified epoch budget (e.g., 100 epochs) and that the final training and validation losses are not statistically higher than those of the original model.

- **Validation:**  
  - Hold out a test set exclusively from the new data distribution.  
  - Evaluate the retrained model on this hold‑out set.  
  - Acceptance criteria:  
    - \(\Delta\text{AUC} < 0.02\) relative to the original cleared model evaluated on its own test set.  
    - Sensitivity for referable DR ≥ 90 % at the calibrated threshold.  
    - Subgroup performance (age, sex, if available) does not exhibit statistically significant degradation in any subgroup compared to the original model.

- **Impact Assessment:**  
  - **Impact level:** High. Retraining alters the model weights and may shift the decision boundary. If the new data distribution differs substantially from the original (e.g., different ethnic populations, different fundus cameras), a prospective multi‑centre study may be recommended by the FDA. In the absence of such a recommendation in the initial clearance, a well‑powered retrospective study on at least two independent, geographically diverse datasets will be conducted.

#### PCCP‑4: Architecture Update (ViT‑Tiny)

- **Verification:**  
  - Replace ViT‑Small/16 with ViT‑Tiny (patch size 16) in the OCT‑A encoding branch.  
  - Apply quantisation‑aware training and confirm that the INT8 model size does not exceed 10 MB.  
  - Measure inference latency on the Jetson AGX Orin target; confirm latency ≤ 30 ms.

- **Validation:**  
  - Re‑run the full EyePACS validation protocol (AUC, sensitivity, specificity, calibration error) as described in Section 6.4.  
  - Acceptance criterion: \(\Delta\text{AUC} < 0.02\) compared to the cleared ViT‑Small/16 configuration, and all other performance metrics remain within their original acceptance limits.

- **Impact Assessment:**  
  - **Impact level:** High. An architecture change constitutes a significant modification. Full re‑demonstration of substantial equivalence is required, but because the change is limited to one encoder and the multimodal fusion remains identical, the existing test datasets and metrics are sufficient. No new clinical study is proposed, but the FDA may request additional data if the performance margin is small.

### C.4 Methodology for Validation

All validation uses the same publicly available datasets (EyePACS, MESSIDOR‑2, OCTA‑500, DM1‑156) and the same evaluation metrics (AUC, sensitivity at 95 % specificity, expected calibration error) as the original clearance. Code, model weights, and the FracEye‑Public feature set are version‑controlled using Git and archived. Validation reports are generated automatically by a scripted evaluation pipeline and included in the 510(k) modification submission.

### C.5 Impact Level Classification Rationale

The impact levels follow FDA’s guidance on PCCP:  
- **Low:** The modification does not alter the algorithm’s core logic and is unlikely to affect safety or effectiveness.  
- **Moderate:** The modification changes an algorithmic parameter or feature definition but does not fundamentally alter the model architecture. Retrospective validation is sufficient.  
- **High:** The modification changes the model weights or architecture and could affect performance. A full re‑validation is required; prospective studies are recommended if the data distribution shifts significantly.

### C.6 Post‑Modification Monitoring

After any PCCP modification is deployed, the post‑market monitoring plan described in Section 7.2.3 will be updated to track the specific modification. For 90 days following release, the rate of `UNRELIABLE` flags and clinician overrides will be compared to baseline; a statistically significant increase triggers an investigation. All incidents are reported under the FDA’s Medical Device Reporting (MDR) regulation and, in the EU, under the vigilance system of the MDR.


